WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.897
Safe Rust gives strong guarantees around
memory access, aliasing, lifetimes,

2
00:00:03.977 --> 00:00:05.143
and data races in safe code.

3
00:00:05.223 --> 00:00:08.265
Those guarantees let developers build
abstractions with confidence.

4
00:00:08.345 --> 00:00:09.866
When a value is borrowed immutably,

5
00:00:09.946 --> 00:00:12.126
safe Rust limits mutation through that
reference.

6
00:00:12.206 --> 00:00:13.574
When a reference has a lifetime,

7
00:00:13.654 --> 00:00:16.443
the compiler checks that it does not
outlive the data it points to.

8
00:00:16.523 --> 00:00:18.399
Unsafe does not turn off the whole
language.

9
00:00:18.479 --> 00:00:21.622
Rust still has types, ownership,
borrowing, pattern matching, modules,

10
00:00:21.702 --> 00:00:24.794
visibility, and ordinary compiler checks
around the unsafe operation.

11
00:00:24.874 --> 00:00:28.575
What unsafe does is permit specific
operations whose correctness depends on promises

12
00:00:28.655 --> 00:00:30.379
the compiler cannot verify completely.

13
00:00:30.459 --> 00:00:32.031
The distinction is responsibility.

14
00:00:32.111 --> 00:00:34.291
Safe code relies heavily on compiler
enforcement.

15
00:00:34.371 --> 00:00:37.514
Unsafe code relies on a combination of
compiler checks, human reasoning,

16
00:00:37.594 --> 00:00:39.825
documented invariants, tests, and careful
review.

17
00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:43.504
The programmer must know which rule is
being manually upheld and why the surrounding

18
00:00:43.584 --> 00:00:44.395
code preserves it.

19
00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:47.416
Undefined behavior should be understood at
a safe conceptual level:

20
00:00:47.496 --> 00:00:50.791
it is behavior the program must avoid
because the language no longer promises

21
00:00:50.871 --> 00:00:51.834
predictable results.

22
00:00:51.914 --> 00:00:55.362
Defensive unsafe Rust does not explore
undefined behavior or treat it as a clever

23
00:00:55.442 --> 00:00:55.695
edge.

24
00:00:55.775 --> 00:00:59.071
It designs code so the documented
preconditions keep execution within valid

25
00:00:59.151 --> 00:00:59.607
behavior.

26
00:00:59.687 --> 00:01:03.185
Review expectations are higher around
unsafe code because the evidence burden is

27
00:01:03.265 --> 00:01:03.620
higher.

28
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.032
Reviewers should ask what safe Rust could
not express,

29
00:01:06.112 --> 00:01:09.205
what assumptions are being made, how those
assumptions are documented,

30
00:01:09.285 --> 00:01:12.580
how callers are constrained, and how tests
or tools support the safety story.
